    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d97">

                <pb facs="0266" n="224" sameAs="frus:frus1951v04p1/pg224"/>

                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#center">No. 97</seg>
                </opener>
                <note rend="inline" type="source"> ECA message files, FRC 53A278, Paris:
                    Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Administrator for Economic Cooperation (</hi>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">Foster</hi>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">) to the United States Special Representative in Europe
                        (</hi>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">Katz</hi>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">), at Paris</hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">confidential</hi>
                    </seg>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Washington</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1951-05-03T20:00:00-04:00">May 3, 1951—8
                            p.m.</date>
                    </dateline>
                </opener>
                <p> Torep 2951. Reference—Repto 1852.<anchor n="1" xml:id="d97-f1"/> Review Trieste
                    question indicates variety of persons with varying degrees of interest have <pb
                        facs="0267" n="225" xml:id="pg225"/> helped make U.S. position maintenance
                    Trieste Mission quite unclear. This cable attempts set forth current State-ECA
                    view for consideration and appropriate action OSR.</p>
                <p>All concerned agree advisability reducing Trieste aid and size Mission; however,
                    Perkins feels politically important continue ECA representation in Trieste
                    independent of ECA/Rome. Feel desirable accede to Perkins’ view unless you have
                    strong objection.</p>
                <p>Projected ECA work Trieste will presumably require one fairly high level person
                    for PA’s screening requirements, follow-up Trieste production (especially
                    ships), general review economic situation and supervision remaining counterpart.
                    Because special nature these tasks feel shld continue to be directed by ECA
                    appointee with status of ECA Mission Chief.</p>
                <p>Would like emphasize wish to follow your judgment size of supporting staff and
                    extent to which OSR might provide direct support so as to minimize staff
                    requirements Trieste.</p>
                <p>Our present information Galloway prepared return unless other assignment
                        available.<anchor n="2" xml:id="d97-f2"/>
                </p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Foster</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d97-f1" n="1" xml:id="d97-f1-content">
                <p>This telegram requested information about the decision concerning whether or not
                    the ECA Mission in Trieste would continue. (ECA message files, FRC 53A278,
                    Paris)</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d97-f2" n="2" xml:id="d97-f2-content">
                <p>In telegram Repto 2237 from Paris, May 15, Katz informed the ECA that he
                    concurred with the Department of State view that it was necessary to maintain
                    the ECA Mission in Trieste. (ECA message files, FRC 53A278, Paris)</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
